Setting: G = GL(2) over a base S (ring A), with rho and sigma as on the previous page. In the case of interest, Tr rho = 1, det rho = 1 (so rho^3 = -1, rho has order 6 and its image in PSL2 has order 3), and Tr sigma = 0, det sigma = 1 (so sigma^2 = -1, sigma has order 4 and its image has order 2). These are the standard generators of SL2(Z), mapping to the free-product generators of PSL2(Z) = Z/2 * Z/3. The one-parameter unipotent subgroup is eps1(lambda) = [[1,0],[lambda,1]] = 1 + lambda N, with N nilpotent, and eps1 = eps1(1) = rho sigma.

The system studied is a simplified form of the 'loop equation' (32). Its unknowns are xi, eta in G and mu, subject to:
(33) xi = eta eps1(mu-1);
(34) det xi = det eta = 1;
(35) Tr(xi rho) = Tr rho and Tr(eta sigma) = Tr sigma.

Eliminating eta = xi eps1(1-mu) gives (36):
- det xi = 1;
- Tr(xi rho) = Tr rho;
- Tr((xi eps1(1-mu) - 1) sigma) = 0.

Since eps1(1-mu) = 1 + (1-mu)N, the third relation is affine in mu and in the entries of xi. Writing it as P(xi) mu - Q(xi) = 0 gives:
- P(xi) = -Tr(xi N sigma);
- -Q(xi) = value at mu = 0, namely Tr((xi eps1 - 1) sigma) = Tr(xi eps1 sigma) - Tr sigma.

Grothendieck then assumes (40) eps1 sigma = -lambda0 rho with lambda0 a central unit, i.e. (40 bis) eps1 = -lambda0 rho sigma^{-1}. Taking determinants gives lambda0^2 = 1 when det rho = det sigma = 1, so lambda0 = ±1 over a domain.

Using the second relation in (36): Tr(xi eps1 sigma) = -lambda0 Tr(xi rho) = -lambda0 Tr rho. Hence Q(xi) = lambda0 Tr rho + Tr sigma =: c, a constant independent of xi; this is (41) on the next page. If c is a unit, mu = c/P(xi) is uniquely determined by xi and is automatically invertible. In the case of interest, lambda0 = 1 and c = 1.

Purpose: parametrize the solution scheme of the GL2 'loop equation', a step in the 'variations sur l'équation des lacets'. These are tied to the Galois–Teichmüller / Longue Marche programme, where PSL2(Z) and the elements of orders 2 and 3 appear.
